y assume a dyadic relationship: one human principal authorizing and supervising one artificial agent. Deployed practice has moved on. Contemporary agentic systems decompose tasks and delegate them onward, to sub-agents they instantiate, to third-party agents discovered through interoperability protocols, and to tools whose own behavior is agentic. Each hop in such a delegation chain attenuates the three prerequisites on which accountability depends: authorized scope, attributable intent, and reconstructable provenance. This study analyzes the resulting accountability gap. We contribute a taxonomy of delegation structures in deployed agentic systems; an analysis of four failure modes by which delegation dissolves accountability, which we term authorization dilution, intent drift, provenance loss, and responsibility laundering; a normative framework, the Delegation Accountabil

